Monoclonal Gammopathy of Undetermined Significance (MGUS)
Haematology and Oncology
Completely asymptomatic presentation, Incidental discovery on blood screening, Absence of CRAB criteria, Serum M-protein less than 30 g/L

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: HMO-031
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe palpitations, chest tightness, and hyperventilation secondary to extreme health anxiety following an automated electronic pathology result notification.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing sudden chest tightness, dyspnoea, and a racing heart after reading a portal notification indicating an abnormal protein. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to clinically rule out an acute coronary syndrome driven by severe panic and to prevent an immediate, inappropriate Emergency Department presentation, confirming the assessment could not be safely delayed until the morning clinic.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My chest is tight, my heart will not stop pounding, and I read my portal results saying I have a blood protein linked to bone cancer."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden, linear progression of severe anxiety symptoms commencing 2 hours ago immediately after receiving a routine blood screening result on their patient portal. The result flagged an incidental serum monoclonal protein. The patient consequently developed a racing heart, hyperventilation, and localized chest tightness. They are otherwise completely asymptomatic. They exhibit a complete absence of unexplained deep, dull bone pain, no history of pathological fragility fractures, no urinary symptoms to suggest nephropathy, and no fatigue or recurrent infections.
Functional Status: Pacing the room, highly agitated.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute psychological distress and risk of iatrogenic panic cascade.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused adult in obvious psychological distress, hyperventilating slightly.
Physical Exam:
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. No murmurs. Heart rate settling with reassurance.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheeze or crackles.
Musculoskeletal: Full, painless range of motion of the spine and pelvis. Complete absence of bony tenderness on palpation.
Abdomen: Soft, non-tender. No palpable hepatosplenomegaly.
Lymphatic: Complete absence of significant cervical, axillary, or inguinal lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Laptop open on the dining table displaying a pathology portal.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. No recent NSAID or analgesic use.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Monoclonal Gammopathy of Undetermined Significance (MGUS) - Status: Stable (Accompanied by Acute Panic Attack)
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the chest tightness resolving completely with structured breathing, absence of radiation, and symptoms directly triggered by acute psychological shock.

Multiple Myeloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the complete absence of CRAB criteria (no bone pain, no clinical anaemia, normal renal function clinically), supporting a purely asymptomatic MGUS presentation.

Smoldering Multiple Myeloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the clinical picture remaining completely asymptomatic, though formal specialist review of the absolute M-protein concentration is required to definitively distinguish.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced an acute panic attack mimicking a cardiovascular event triggered by the incidental electronic discovery of a serum monoclonal protein. Objective clinical assessment confirms an absence of bone tenderness, pallor, or organomegaly, satisfying the clinical criteria for a completely asymptomatic presentation characteristic of MGUS. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in escalating severe panic and an unnecessary, resource-intensive emergency department presentation for suspected cardiac chest pain. The urgent home visit was critical to immediately rule out acute coronary ischaemia, establish haemodynamic stability, and deliver definitive clinical reassurance regarding the benign nature of the incidental finding prior to routine specialist follow-up.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided. Box breathing exercises supervised to completely reverse the hyperventilation and tachycardia. Referral generated for routine, non-urgent haematology outpatient review to confirm the serum M-protein is less than 30 g/L and formally exclude progression.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Disable automated electronic notifications for pathology results on the patient portal tonight. By preventing the immediate, unfiltered delivery of complex medical data, you remove the trigger for extreme health anxiety and allow your doctor to interpret results in context.

Complete five minutes of structured box breathing before checking blood pressure. By consciously regulating your respiratory rate, you directly lower your heart rate and prevent anxiety-induced hypertension.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to discuss the formal pathology report.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you develop central, crushing chest pain that radiates to your jaw or arm, if you experience sudden breathlessness at rest that does not respond to breathing exercises, or if you faint.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: The physical examination shows no signs of active bone disease, but routine specialist blood tests are required to confirm the exact levels of this incidental protein.
Clinical Course: The abnormal protein is an incidental finding that carries a very low but measurable ongoing risk of progression (approximately 1 percent per year), requiring simple yearly monitoring rather than immediate treatment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
